---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import PageHero from "../components/PageHero.astro";
import BenchBadge from "../components/BenchBadge.astro";
import BenchFootnote from "../components/BenchFootnote.astro";
import { SITE } from "../data/site";
import Linked from "../components/Linked.astro";
const seen = new Set<string>();

type Status = "shipped" | "planned";
const label: Record<Status, string> = { shipped: "Shipped", planned: "Planned" };
const pillClass: Record<Status, string> = { shipped: "pill pill-ok", planned: "pill pill-planned" };

const pillars: { id: string; title: string; status: Status; points: string[] }[] = [
  {
    id: "transport",
    title: "TLS 1.3 on every media connection",
    status: "shipped",
    points: [
      "QUIC and WebTransport carry TLS 1.3 as part of the protocol, so no media connection is ever in plaintext.",
      "Raw QUIC and WebTransport share one UDP port, so there's only one surface to harden and monitor.",
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "e2ee",
    title: "Media payload encryption with SFrame",
    status: "shipped",
    points: [
      "Tracks are encrypted on the device with SFrame (RFC 9605). AES-256-GCM is the default and AES-128-GCM is supported. This is the approach the IETF MoQ working group is standardising in draft-ietf-moq-secure-objects.",
      "Only the payload is encrypted, so the relay can still cache, deduplicate and prioritise what it can't read.",
      "Encrypting a 1080p keyframe on an iPhone takes about 54 µs*, against a 33 ms frame budget.",
      "Required in every room. There is no setting that turns it off, for you or for us, and the SDK drops a frame rather than send it unencrypted.",
      "Keys are managed end to end with MLS (RFC 9420). Everyone who may publish is a member of the room's group, every member sees every other, and a device that leaves can't read what comes next.",
      "Large audiences use sender keys: viewers aren't added to the group. Each viewer's device makes its own ML-KEM-1024 key pair, the group seals every epoch's key to it with HPKE over ML-KEM-1024, and the epoch rotates every five minutes, so a viewer who leaves stops being able to read.",
      "Catalogs are sealed by their publisher too. The relay is given only what it needs to route: each track's name, kind and size.",
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "post-quantum",
    title: "Post-quantum, end to end",
    status: "shipped",
    points: [
      "An attacker who records encrypted calls today and gets a quantum computer later still can't open them. Every layer that agrees a key does it with post-quantum cryptography.",
      "Authentication is post-quantum too, not just secrecy. Every identity key, device credential, key package, commit and message signature is ML-DSA-87, so a quantum computer can't be used to impersonate anyone either.",
      "End-to-end keys: every room's MLS group uses ML-KEM-1024 for key agreement and ML-DSA-87 for signatures, NIST's post-quantum standards (FIPS 203 and 204), with AES-256-GCM and SHA-512. That is MLS_256_MLKEM1024_AES256GCM_SHA512_MLDSA87, the category-5 suite from the IETF's draft-ietf-mls-pq-ciphersuites: every part of it is in NIST's highest security category. It's the only suite, so there's nothing to negotiate and nothing to turn on.",
      "Media: SFrame with AES-256-GCM. A 256-bit symmetric key keeps about 128 bits of security against a quantum computer, so media encryption didn't have to change, and neither did its 54 µs cost.",
      "Transport: every device connection uses ML-KEM-1024 key exchange in TLS 1.3 and nothing else: media to relays, sign-in and token renewal, and room key delivery. The Swift SDK connects through our own QUIC and TLS library (rustls with AWS-LC), so the SDK chooses the key exchange, not the operating system. Links between our own servers are ML-KEM-1024 only too. Classical-only key exchange is refused everywhere.",
      "api.moqom.cloud, which serves billing, the console and the Go SDK, runs on our own TLS terminator: ML-KEM-1024 preferred, the Go SDK requires the P-384 hybrid SecP384r1MLKEM1024, and browsers get hybrid X25519MLKEM768, the strongest any browser offers.",
      "Standard algorithms only. We didn't invent any cryptography: on devices every algorithm comes from Apple's CryptoKit, the control plane uses Go's standard library, and relays use AWS-LC.",
      "Tokens: join tokens are signed with ML-DSA-87 and sealed with AES-256-GCM, and the room's control log is signed with ML-DSA-87 too, the record of who is on stage and what is attached. Devices ignore any entry that doesn't verify.",
      "Devices: the key that ties a token to its phone is an ML-DSA-87 key held in the Secure Enclave, and every proof the phone makes with it is post-quantum.",
      "Still classical, stated plainly: certificate signatures in TLS. Relay certificates and our Let's Encrypt certificates are ECDSA or RSA, because the WebPKI has no post-quantum certificates yet. Phones accept only the exact relay certificate they were given, and media is end-to-end encrypted regardless, so forging one would need a working quantum computer during the call, not a recording of it.",
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "one-to-one",
    title: "1:1 calls: point to point, the most private thing we run",
    status: "shipped",
    points: [
      "Exactly two devices can decrypt a 1:1 call. A broadcast shares its keys with every viewer it lets in, and nobody else.",
      "Both people can compare the call's epoch authenticator on screen. If it matches, nobody else is in the group.",
      "MOQOM's servers can't record what they can't read: anything stored on our side is ciphertext and stays that way. A stolen token is useless without the phone's Secure Enclave key.",
      "1:1 calls are point to point: media goes phone to phone and never reaches a MOQOM server, so there's nothing on our side to breach or log. Where a network blocks a direct path, the call carries on through a relay, still end-to-end encrypted.",
      "Point to point means the two phones learn each other's IP address. The app says so before the call, and an app can keep any call on the relay instead.",
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "tokens",
    title: "Tokens bound to the device's hardware key",
    status: "shipped",
    points: [
      "Client tokens are ML-DSA-87-signed, short-lived and scoped to a tenant, a room and an explicit set of grants.",
      "Every token is then sealed with AES-256-GCM under a key only our control plane and relays hold. To your app, your backend and anything that logs it, a token is opaque bytes: it says nothing about who holds it or what it grants.",
      "Stronger than DPoP (RFC 9449), which it builds on. Every token is bound to an ML-DSA-87 key that never leaves the device's Secure Enclave. The control plane won't issue a token that isn't, and relays refuse any session that can't prove it holds that key. A token lifted from logs or from memory doesn't work from anywhere else.",
      "Each proof is single-use and tied to the exact relay or phone it's sent to, by that connection's TLS certificate. A proof captured on the way can't be replayed, and can't be passed through a machine in the middle. See how it compares with DPoP below.",
      "Tokens refresh automatically: the SDK fetches the next one from your backend two minutes before expiry and hands it to the relay mid-session, without reconnecting or dropping the call.",
      "Every publish, subscribe and fetch is checked against the session's token, whether it carries audio, video, screen share or data. A token that lapses without renewal closes the session.",
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "domains",
    title: "Domains that resist registry hijacks",
    status: "shipped",
    points: [
      "Phones accept only the exact certificates they were given for relays and the token service, and never ask a certificate authority. A certificate obtained by hijacking DNS, or issued by mistake, can't intercept a call or collect a token.",
      "CAA records allow only two certificate authorities to issue for our domains, and no one to issue a wildcard.",
      "Our domains are DNSSEC-signed and locked against transfer and update at the registrar.",
      "Certificate Transparency is checked every night. Any certificate for our names that our own load balancers don't hold raises a critical alert.",
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "open-source",
    title: "Open-source security libraries",
    status: "shipped",
    points: [
      "The code that keeps MOQOM private is public: moqom-token, moqom-proof, moqom-sframe and moqom-mls, each in Swift, Go and Rust, under Apache-2.0.",
      "It's the code our apps, relays and control plane run, not a separate copy. Every language passes one shared set of test vectors, including the RFC 9605 vectors.",
      "Each library composes its platform's audited primitives (CryptoKit, Go's standard library, AWS-LC) and implements none of its own.",
      "Source, documentation and vectors: github.com/moqom-cloud/moqom-security.",
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "revocation",
    title: "Bans take effect mid-session",
    status: "shipped",
    points: [
      "Kick or ban someone through the admin API and their open connection closes. You don't have to wait for their token to expire.",
      "Unbanning someone and closing a room take effect the same way. An end-to-end test covers this: a real control plane, a real relay and a real client.",
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "mtls",
    title: "Mutual TLS between relays and the control plane",
    status: "shipped",
    points: [
      "Relays and the control plane authenticate each other over TLS 1.3 with client certificates. Certificates are required, not just requested.",
      "Each relay also presents its own credential, so a stolen credential alone is not enough to pose as a relay.",
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "isolation",
    title: "Tenant isolation and quotas",
    status: "shipped",
    points: [
      "Every request is checked against the tenant, room and grants in its token, for every namespace it names.",
      "Per-device and per-user quotas apply to sessions, subscriptions, announcements, watches and fetches.",
      "Twenty-one capabilities, such as publishing, moderating or bringing someone on stage, are granted one by one and verified across both backend SDKs and the server.",
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "agents",
    title: "Recorders and classifiers that everyone can see",
    status: "planned",
    points: [
      "Encryption never has to be turned off for recording or moderation. A recorder or classifier you run joins the room's group as a member, on your side, and decrypts there. MOQOM's servers still see only ciphertext.",
      "It can't join unannounced. Every member sees every other, and each device holds back its own media while the group contains an agent that the room's signed control log hasn't disclosed.",
      "Built and tested in the Swift SDK and the control plane. Planned for hosted rooms until the control plane publishes the signed log there.",
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "p2p",
    title: "Point to point for 1:1 calls and small private rooms",
    status: "shipped",
    points: [
      "1:1 calls go point to point by default, and private rooms of up to about four people can too. Media goes directly between devices, and the relay carries none of it.",
      "Rooms of three or four go direct only when every participant agrees, and the interface tells them that peers will see each other's IP address. Never while a recorder or classifier is in the room.",
      "Never offered to an audience or for public broadcasts. It falls back to the relay when a direct path isn't available.",
      "The handshake: each phone seals its addresses and a one-call certificate fingerprint under the room's MLS key, so the relay can't read or forge them. The phones hole-punch, pin each other's certificate, then each presents a direct ticket, signed by the relay and renewed about once a minute, plus a single-use ML-DSA-87 Secure Enclave signature bound to that ticket and to the other phone's certificate. Phones never see each other's tokens. Each side checks the other exactly as a relay would: right tenant and room, allowed to publish, the person whose offer was accepted. Either side can refuse before any media moves.",
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "hygiene",
    title: "Engineering hygiene",
    status: "shipped",
    points: [
      "Our own implementation of the MoQ transport draft, with coverage-guided fuzzing of every decoder on every change.",
      "The QUIC stack is vendored unmodified, and a script verifies it against upstream.",
      "The relay's admin and metrics port is off by default and bound separately from media.",
    ],
  },
];

const pqAuthCompare = [
  ["Recorded messages stay secret against a future quantum computer", "Yes: ML-KEM-1024 in MLS, AES-256 for media", "Yes: PQXDH and the SPQR post-quantum ratchet"],
  ["Long-term identity key", "ML-DSA-87, made in the Secure Enclave", "Curve25519, classical"],
  ["Prekeys and key packages signed with", "ML-DSA-87", "XEdDSA, classical"],
  ["Group changes and messages signed with", "ML-DSA-87, by the member who made them", "Not signed per message; sessions are authenticated by the classical identity keys"],
  ["Impersonating a contact with a future quantum computer", "Resisted: the signing key can't be derived from the public key", "Possible in new sessions, and the safety number still matches, because the public key is the real one"],
];

const dpopCompare = [
  ["Binding", "Required. No token is issued without a device key, and relays accept no bearer tokens", "Optional. A server can still accept plain bearer tokens"],
  ["Where the key lives", "The Secure Enclave, where it can't be exported", "Left to the client; often software or browser storage"],
  ["What a proof is tied to", "The token and the TLS certificate of the exact relay or phone receiving it", "The HTTP method and URL. Not the connection, so a proof works through a machine in the middle"],
  ["Replay", "Each proof is accepted once", "Reusable within the server's time window unless it tracks every proof it has seen"],
  ["Signature", "ML-DSA-87, post-quantum", "Whatever JWS algorithm the client picks; classical in practice"],
  ["Long sessions", "Renewed inside the open session, without reconnecting", "A fresh proof with every HTTP request"],
  ["Peer to peer", "Phones check each other with the relay's own rules", "Client to server only"],
];

const sframeSpeed = [
  {
    title: "Hardware AES, in native code",
    body: "AES-256-GCM runs on the chip's AES instructions through Apple's CryptoKit, in native code, on the same device that captures and encodes the frame.",
  },
  {
    title: "One cipher call per frame",
    body: "Each sender's key and salt are derived once per key change, and nonces come from a counter. Per frame, all that's left is a single AES-GCM operation: no key derivation and no random numbers.",
  },
  {
    title: "A fixed 19 bytes",
    body: "Header and tag together add 19 bytes per object, whatever its size: 0.9% of a 2 KB video object.",
  },
  {
    title: "Relays can't decrypt",
    body: "Relays are blind to the encryption: they're never given the keys, so they can't decrypt a frame, however many a frame crosses. Only the payload is sealed, so they still cache, prioritise and fan out the ciphertext as is.",
  },
  {
    title: "Tamper-proofing for free",
    body: "Each object's group, position, priority and capture time are bound into the authentication tag. That costs a few bytes of hashing and stops a relay reordering, relabelling or replaying frames.",
  },
  {
    title: "Post-quantum keys, agreed off the frame path",
    body: "The keys come from MLS with ML-KEM and ML-DSA. That work happens when someone joins or leaves, never per frame, so each frame costs one AES-GCM operation whatever the key exchange.",
  },
];

const sframeCost = [
  ["60-byte message or Opus packet", "3.2 µs", "3.2 µs"],
  ["2 KB video delta", "4.0 µs", "3.8 µs"],
  ["40 KB 720p keyframe", "15.1 µs", "12.0 µs"],
  ["200 KB 1080p keyframe", "46.9 µs", "43.6 µs"],
];

const sframeCompare = [
  ["MOQOM", "Always on; can't be turned off", "Native, hardware AES", "Ciphertext only; frame position and priority authenticated"],
  ["Agora", "Built-in media encryption opt-in; E2EE in beta (web, by request)", "Not stated", "Not stated"],
  ["Tencent RTC", "Private payload encryption opt-in", "Not stated", "Not available with cloud recording or CDN relay"],
];

const interception = [
  {
    title: "No one in the middle",
    points: [
      "QUIC has no unencrypted mode and no way to negotiate TLS down, so every connection is TLS 1.3 from the first packet.",
      "Phones accept a relay only if its certificate is the exact one they were given. A valid certificate from any authority isn't enough.",
      "Relays and the control plane authenticate each other with mutual TLS, so an impostor can't pose as either one.",
      "A stolen token is bound to the device it was issued to, and is useless anywhere else.",
    ],
  },
  {
    title: "No one listening in",
    points: [
      "TLS 1.3 uses fresh keys for every connection (forward secrecy), so recorded traffic stays unreadable even if a server key leaks later. The key exchange is ML-KEM-1024 on every device connection and every link between our own servers, and classical-only key exchange is refused.",
      "Media, data and catalogs are sealed on the device with SFrame in every room, and nothing turns that off. Relays, MOQOM and the cloud underneath see only ciphertext.",
      "Tampering with an encrypted object's position, priority or properties makes it fail authentication, so it is dropped rather than played.",
    ],
  },
  {
    title: "Why we pin certificates",
    points: [
      "A certificate authority issues to whoever controls a domain's DNS, and DNS is only as safe as the registry above it. Pinning takes registries and authorities out of the question of who a relay is.",
      "Pins are delivered with the address, in the link that sets up a phone, so a certificate change is a new link, not an app update.",
      "Self-hosted deployments choose in the Swift SDK: exact pinning, which takes several hashes so rotation has no gap, or system trust for relays with a public certificate.",
    ],
  },
];

const compare = [
  ["Transport encryption", "TLS 1.3 on every connection", "Required, because MoQ runs over QUIC or WebTransport"],
  ["Authorization", "Signed, scoped, short-lived tokens checked on every request", "Defines how a token is carried. What it contains and how it's checked are left to the deployment"],
  ["Media payload encryption", "SFrame, AES-256-GCM, required in every room", "Specified separately and optional: draft-ietf-moq-secure-objects"],
  ["Post-quantum protection", "ML-KEM-1024 and ML-DSA-87 for end-to-end keys, ML-DSA-87 tokens and device proofs, ML-KEM-1024 key exchange on every device connection and server link, AES-256 media", "Not specified"],
  ["Token bound to the device", "Every token, to an ML-DSA-87 key in the Secure Enclave, enforced by every relay", "Not specified"],
  ["Revoking a live session", "A ban closes the open connection", "Not specified. Left to the application"],
  ["Relay ↔ control-plane auth", "Mutual TLS 1.3, plus a per-relay credential", "Out of scope"],
  ["Multi-tenant isolation", "Tenant, room and grants checked for every namespace", "Out of scope"],
];
---

<Base
  title="Security"
  description="Post-quantum end-to-end encryption in every room, with no way to turn it off (SFrame, with keys agreed by MLS using ML-KEM and ML-DSA), TLS 1.3 everywhere, hardware-bound tokens, in-session revocation, mutual TLS between relays and the control plane, and tenant isolation."
>
  <PageHero
    eyebrow="Security"
    title="End-to-end encrypted, always. Precise about what that means."
    lede="MOQOM builds in security work that would otherwise fall to your application. Every item below is labelled shipped or planned, so you know exactly what you're getting today."
  />

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <nav class="toc" aria-label="On this page">
        {pillars.map((p) => <a href={`#${p.id}`}>{p.title}</a>)}
        <a href="#dpop">Tokens vs DPoP</a>
        <a href="#sframe">Why SFrame is fast</a>
        <a href="#interception">Interception</a>
        <a href="#compare">Comparison</a>
      </nav>
      <div class="pillars">
        {
          pillars.map((p) => (
            <article class="glass card pillar reveal" id={p.id}>
              <div class="pillar-head">
                <h2>{p.title}</h2>
                <span class={pillClass[p.status]}>{label[p.status]}</span>
              </div>
              <ul class="check-list">
                {p.points.map((pt) => (
                  <li class="muted"><Linked text={pt} seen={seen} /></li>
                ))}
              </ul>
              {p.id === "e2ee" && <BenchBadge />}
              {p.id === "e2ee" && <BenchFootnote />}
            </article>
          ))
        }
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" id="pq-authentication">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-head reveal">
        <span class="eyebrow">Post-quantum authentication</span>
        <h2>Quantum-safe against impersonation, not just eavesdropping</h2>
        <p class="lede">
          A quantum computer that breaks elliptic curves can work out a private key from its public key. Post-quantum
          encryption stops it reading what was recorded. It doesn't stop it signing as you. Signal's identity keys and
          signatures are Curve25519 and XEdDSA, which are classical: a future quantum computer couldn't read recorded
          Signal messages, but it could impersonate a contact or forge identity keys. In MOQOM every identity key, device
          credential, key package, commit and message signature is ML-DSA-87 (FIPS 204), so impersonation is
          quantum-resistant too.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="glass glass--strong table-wrap">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr><th>Concern</th><th>MOQOM</th><th>Signal</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {
              pqAuthCompare.map(([c, m, d]) => (
                <tr>
                  <th scope="row">{c}</th>
                  <td class="col-moqom"><Linked text={m} seen={seen} /></td>
                  <td class="muted"><Linked text={d} seen={seen} /></td>
                </tr>
              ))
            }
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p class="small faint note">
        Still classical, stated plainly: certificate signatures in TLS (the WebPKI has no post-quantum certificates yet), and Apple's signatures on Sign in with
        Apple and App Attest. Forging Apple's could at most enroll a new device, which joins the room as a new ML-DSA-87 key
        every member sees; it can't sign as an existing one. Signal column: our reading of Signal's published
        <a href="https://signal.org/docs/specifications/pqxdh/" rel="noopener">PQXDH specification</a>, which says its
        authentication relies on classical assumptions, as of October 2026.
        <a href="https://moqom.dev/security/post-quantum/">How it works →</a>
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" id="dpop">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-head reveal">
        <span class="eyebrow">Device-bound tokens</span>
        <h2>Why MOQOM tokens are stronger than DPoP</h2>
        <p class="lede">
          DPoP (<a href="https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9449" rel="noopener">RFC 9449</a>) showed how to tie a token to a key. MOQOM keeps its shape and closes what it leaves open: the
          binding is mandatory, the key is in hardware, each proof is used once and tied to the connection it's sent on,
          and the signature is post-quantum.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="glass glass--strong table-wrap">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr><th>Concern</th><th>MOQOM tokens</th><th>DPoP (RFC 9449)</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {
              dpopCompare.map(([c, m, d]) => (
                <tr>
                  <th scope="row">{c}</th>
                  <td class="col-moqom"><Linked text={m} seen={seen} /></td>
                  <td class="muted"><Linked text={d} seen={seen} /></td>
                </tr>
              ))
            }
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p class="small faint note">
        DPoP column: our reading of <a href="https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9449" rel="noopener">RFC 9449</a> as published. It describes the standard, not any product that uses it.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" id="sframe">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-head reveal">
        <span class="eyebrow">SFrame</span>
        <h2>Why end-to-end encryption costs almost nothing here</h2>
        <p class="lede">
          Sealing a 1080p keyframe takes about 54 µs on an iPhone*, 0.16% of a 33 ms frame. That's cheap enough to
          require in every room, which is why it can't be turned off. This is where the speed comes from.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="grid grid-3">
        {
          sframeSpeed.map((s) => (
            <div class="glass card reveal">
              <h3>{s.title}</h3>
              <p class="small muted"><Linked text={s.body} seen={seen} /></p>
            </div>
          ))
        }
      </div>
      <div class="glass glass--strong table-wrap sframe-table">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr><th>Object</th><th>Seal</th><th>Open</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {
              sframeCost.map(([o, s, op]) => (
                <tr>
                  <th scope="row">{o}</th>
                  <td class="col-moqom">{s}</td>
                  <td>{op}</td>
                </tr>
              ))
            }
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p class="small faint note">
        Seal and open times measured in a release build on an Apple M1 Pro MacBook Pro, AES-256-GCM, 19 bytes of
        overhead at every size. * The iPhone figure is the on-device measurement on our benchmarks page.
      </p>
      <div class="glass glass--strong table-wrap sframe-table">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr><th>Product</th><th>End-to-end encryption</th><th>Where frames are encrypted</th><th>What the server sees</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {
              sframeCompare.map(([p, e, w, s]) => (
                <tr>
                  <th scope="row">{p}</th>
                  <td class={p === "MOQOM" ? "col-moqom" : "muted"}><Linked text={e} seen={seen} /></td>
                  <td class={p === "MOQOM" ? "col-moqom" : "muted"}><Linked text={w} seen={seen} /></td>
                  <td class={p === "MOQOM" ? "col-moqom" : "muted"}><Linked text={s} seen={seen} /></td>
                </tr>
              ))
            }
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p class="small faint note">
        Agora and Tencent RTC rows are from their own documentation as cited on our <a href="/compare/">comparison
        page</a>, October 2026. "Not stated" means we found no statement, not that the feature is absent.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" id="interception">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-head reveal">
        <span class="eyebrow">Interception</span>
        <h2>Nothing to intercept, and no one to impersonate</h2>
        <p class="lede">
          On the network, a man-in-the-middle or an eavesdropper gets nothing usable from MOQOM. What remains is
          stated plainly: relays can see track names, sizes, timing and each keyframe's codec parameters. They never
          see media, because there is no unencrypted room.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="pillars">
        {
          interception.map((p) => (
            <article class="glass card pillar reveal">
              <div class="pillar-head">
                <h2>{p.title}</h2>
              </div>
              <ul class="check-list">
                {p.points.map((pt) => (
                  <li class="muted"><Linked text={pt} seen={seen} /></li>
                ))}
              </ul>
            </article>
          ))
        }
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" id="compare">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-head reveal">
        <span class="eyebrow">Comparison</span>
        <h2>What MOQOM ships, and what the standard leaves open</h2>
        <p class="lede">
          The IETF MoQ transport draft deliberately leaves policy to the people who deploy it. These are the parts
          MOQOM fills in for you.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="glass glass--strong table-wrap">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr><th>Concern</th><th>MOQOM ships</th><th>IETF draft-ietf-moq-transport</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {
              compare.map(([c, m, d]) => (
                <tr>
                  <th scope="row">{c}</th>
                  <td class="col-moqom"><Linked text={m} seen={seen} /></td>
                  <td class="muted"><Linked text={d} seen={seen} /></td>
                </tr>
              ))
            }
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p class="small faint note">
        Open-source MoQ relays such as moq-rs, Cloudflare's MoQ relay and Meta's moxygen implement versions of the same
        transport drafts. How each handles authorization, payload encryption and revocation is set out in its own
        documentation, and we encourage you to compare it with the list above. This table describes the standard, not
        any other product. It reflects our reading of <a href="https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/draft-ietf-moq-transport-19" rel="noopener">draft-ietf-moq-transport-19</a> as of October 2026.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="narrow">
      <div class="glass card reveal" id="disclosure">
        <h2>Reporting a vulnerability</h2>
        <p class="muted">
          Please report security issues privately to <a href={`mailto:${SITE.security}`}>{SITE.security}</a>, with
          enough detail for us to reproduce them.
        </p>
        <h3>Bug bounty</h3>
        <p class="muted">
          Our HackerOne program launches as a vulnerability disclosure program. Cash bounties are funded by donations to
          the open-source projects until paying customers adopt MOQOM. Until then, every valid report gets public credit
          and our thanks — in the hall of fame and release notes — and we'll send you something: swag in the mail or a
          tip to your crypto wallet.
        </p>
        <!-- TODO: link SITE.hackerone (https://hackerone.com/elliottwave) once the program is live. -->
        <p class="small faint">
          The HackerOne program isn't open yet. Until it is, email <a href={`mailto:${SITE.security}`}>{SITE.security}</a>.
          You can support the open-source projects through <a href={SITE.donate} rel="noopener">donations ↗</a>.
        </p>
        <p class="small faint">For SOC 2, ISO 27001, GDPR and PCI status, and our subprocessors, see <a href="/compliance/">Compliance</a>.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>

